      "output": "Title: Built-in Functions — Python 3.14.7 documentation\nURL: https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n| A `abs()` `aiter()` `all()` `anext()` `any()` `ascii()` B `bin()` `bool()` `breakpoint()` `bytearray()` `bytes()` C `callable()` `chr()` `classmethod()` `compile()` `complex()` D `delattr()` `dict()` `dir()` `divmod()` | E `enumerate()` `eval()` `exec()` F `filter()` `float()` `format()` `frozenset()` G `getattr()` `globals()` H `hasattr()` `hash()` `help()` `hex()` I `id()` `input()` `int()` `isinstance()` `issubclass()` `iter()` | L `len()` `list()` `locals()` M `map()` `max()` `memoryview()` `min()` N `next()` O `object()` `oct()` `open()` `ord()` P `pow()` `print()` `property()` | R `range()` `repr()` `reversed()` `round()` S `set()` `setattr()` `slice()` `sorted()` `staticmethod()` `str()` `sum()` `super()` T `tuple()` `type()` V `vars()` Z `zip()` _ `__import__()` |\n...\nprint(* objects, sep=' ', end='\\n', file\n...\nNone, flush= False)¶\n\n---\n\nTitle: sum() | Python’s Built-in Functions – Real Python\nURL: https://realpython.com/ref/builtin-functions/sum/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: Real Python\nHighlights:\nThe built-in`sum()` function provides a Pythonic way to add together the items of an iterable. It efficiently computes the total sum of numeric values, with the option to include an initial value for the summation process:\n...\n```\n>>> sum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n15\n\n>>> sum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], start=100)\n115\n\n```\n...\n```\nsum(iterable, /, start=0)\n...\n| Argument | Description | Default Value |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `iterable` | Any Python iterable containing numeric values. It can also hold concatenable objects, such as lists and tuples. | Required argument |\n| `start` | An optional argument to initialize the summation. It can be a number, list, or tuple, but not a string. | `0` |\n...\n- The sum of the`start` value and the values in the input iterable from left to right.\n- The return value’s data type matches the type of the elements in the input iterable.\n...\n```\n>>> sum([x ** 2 for x in range(1, 6)])\n55\n...\n```\n>>> sum([1, 2, 3], start=10)\n16\n...\n50, 200],  #\n...\n80, 1\n...\n, 1\n...\n0],   #\n...\n>>> sum(\n...\nsales)\n\n---\n\nTitle: What Is Python's sum() Function and How Do You Use It? - The New Stack\nURL: https://thenewstack.io/what-is-pythons-sum-function-and-how-do-you-use-it/\nPublished: 2024-08-11T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Jack Wallen\nHighlights:\nThe `sum()` function can be used with lists, tuples, sets, ranges and dictionaries.\n...\n- List: sum([3, 5, 7, 10])\n- Tuple: sum((3, 5, 7, 10))\n- Set: sum({3, 5, 7, 10})\n- Range: sum(range(3, 10))\n- Dictionary: sum({3: “three”, 5: “five”, 7: “seven”, 10: “ten”})\n...\nWith the exception of the range, all of the above will total 25. The range, on the other hand, will total 42.\n...\nYou could also get a bit more fancy with your math. For example, let’s say you want to compute the sum of the squares from a range of values. That might look something like this:\n...\n```\nsum([x ** 2 for x in range(1, 10)])\n```\n...\nThe total from\n...\nabove would be 285.\n\n---\n\nTitle: sum() in Python - Built-In Functions with Examples\nURL: https://diveintopython.org/functions/built-in/sum\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe `sum()` function is a built-in function in Python that takes an iterable (such as a list) of numbers as input and returns the sum of all the numbers in the iterable.\n...\nExample 1: \n\nReturn the sum of a 'list' or 'tuple' of numbers.\n...\n```python3\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nresult = sum(numbers)\nprint(result)\n...\n```python3\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n...\ninitial_value = 10\n...\nresult = sum(numbers, initial_value)\nprint(result)\n...\nExample 3: \n\nCalculate the sum of numbers in a 'generator expression'.\n...\n```python3\ngenerator = (x**2 for x in range(5))\nresult = sum(generator)\nprint(result)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Built-in Functions — Python 3.11.5 documentation\nURL: https://docs.python.org/release/3.11.5/library/functions.html\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n`sum()`\n...\nsum(iterable, /, start=0)¶\n...\nand the items of\n...\nfrom left to right\n...\nreturns the total. The iterable\n...\nto be a string.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Python range() Function Explained with Examples\nURL: https://pynative.com/python-range-function/\nPublished: 2022-03-17T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Vishal\nHighlights:\nHow do you sum a range() in Python?\n...\nUse bulit-in function sum(). For example, `sum(range(10)`\n\n---\n\nTitle: Python range() function\nURL: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/python-range-function/\nPublished: 2026-03-10T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe range() function in Python is used to generate a sequence of integers within a specified range. It is most commonly used in loops to control how many times a block of code runs.\n...\nExample: This example shows the use of range() to generate numbers starting from 0 up to (but not including) a given value.\n...\n```\nfor i in range(5):\n    print(i, end=\" \")\n\n```\n\n Output\n...\n```\n0 1 2 3 4 \n```\n...\ngenerates numbers from 0 to 4\n...\ni takes one value\n...\n> range(start, stop, step)\n...\n- start (optional): Starting number of the sequence (default is 0)\n- stop: Number at which the sequence stops (not included)\n- step (optional): Difference between consecutive numbers (default is 1)\n...\nReturn: A range object representing the sequence\n...\nExample 1: This example generates numbers starting from a custom value and ending before another value.\n...\n```\nfor n in range(5, 10):\n    print(n, end=\" \")\n\n```\n...\n```\n5 6 7 8 9 \n```\n...\n- range(5, 10) starts at 5 and stops before 10\n- Numbers increase by the default step of 1\n...\n```\nfor v in range(0, 10, 2):\n    print(v, end=\" \")\n\n```\n...\n```\n0 2 4 6 8 \n```\n...\n- range(0,\n...\n10, 2) increases the value by 2\n...\nExample 3: This example demonstrates\n...\nby providing a negative step value\n...\n```\nfor i in range(10, 0, -2):\n    print(i, end=\" \")\n\n```\n...\n```\n10 8 6 4 2 \n```\n...\n(10,\n...\n0, -2\n...\n10\n\n---\n\nTitle: gh-107868 Add an `O(1)` fastpath for `sum(range(...))` · Pull Request #107870 · python/cpython\nURL: https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/107870\nPublished: 2023-08-11T15:52:11.000Z\nAuthor: mcognetta\nHighlights:\n`, which takes `O(1)` time.\n...\n```python\n>>> import time\n>>> t=time.time();sum(range(1,pow(10,8)+1));print(time.time()-t)\n5000000050000000\n3.165787696838379\n```\n...\n```python\n>>> import time\n>>> t=time.time();sum(range(1,pow(10,8)+1));print(time.time()-t)\n5000000050000000\n7.939338684082031e-05\n```\n\n \n\n- Issue: gh-107868\n\n \n\n---\n...\n> Thanks for your reviews. I will fix the clinic stuff tomorrow.\n> \n> There is one other issue that I am concerned about. When running something like:\n> \n> ```python\n> >>> r = range(2**1000, 2**1000 + 1000, 999)\n> >>> sum(r)\n> \n...\n> >>> r = range(2**64)\n> >>> sum(r)\n> Segmentation fault (core dumped)\n> ```\n> \n> There is a segfault, even though it should be able to represent all of these numbers.\n> \n> Actually, in a prior version of this implementation, doing `sum(range(2**64))` wouldn't segfault, but it would say that the code returned a error as it could not be represented in a `ssize_t` (I don't have the exact error unfortunately).\n> \n> I am not sure what the result would be if you ran that same code on `main`, as it would take too long to finish. But it concerns me that this is clearly able to generate numbers larger than the system max, but in some cases it fails. What do you think?",
